WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:04.560
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute&nbsp;
Papers with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.560 --> 00:00:11.520
If we wish to create an adorable virtual monster&nbsp;
and animate it, we first have to engage in 3D&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.520 --> 00:00:17.360
modeling. Then, if we wish to make it move,&nbsp;
and make this movement believable, we have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.360 --> 00:00:24.160
to specify where the bones and joints are located&nbsp;
within the model. This process is called rigging.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.360 --> 00:00:31.440
As you see, it is quite laborious, and requires&nbsp;
expertise in this domain to pull this off.

00:00:32.240 --> 00:00:40.240
And now, imagine this process with no 3D&nbsp;
modeling, and no rigging. This is, of course,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.240 --> 00:00:47.040
impossible…right? Well, now, you know what’s&nbsp;
coming, so hold on to your papers, because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.040 --> 00:00:53.120
here is a newer technique that indeed performs&nbsp;
the impossible. All we need to do is grab a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.120 --> 00:01:00.560
pencil and create a rough sketch of our character,&nbsp;
then, it will take a big breath, and inflate it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.560 --> 00:01:07.440
into a 3D model. This process was nearly 7 times&nbsp;
faster than the classical workflow, but what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.440 --> 00:01:14.240
matters even more, this new workflow requires&nbsp;
zero expertise in 3D modeling and rigging.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.600 --> 00:01:19.520
With this technique, absolutely&nbsp;
anybody can become an artist.

00:01:20.320 --> 00:01:27.200
So, we noted that these models can also be&nbsp;
animated. Is that so? Yes, that’s right,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.200 --> 00:01:33.120
we can indeed animate these models by using&nbsp;
these red control points. And even better,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.120 --> 00:01:38.960
we get to specify where these points go. That’s&nbsp;
a good thing because we can make sure that a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.960 --> 00:01:45.280
prescribed part can move around, opening up&nbsp;
the possibility of creating and animating&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.280 --> 00:01:50.800
a wide range of characters. And I would say&nbsp;
all this can be done in a matter of minutes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.440 --> 00:01:56.960
but even better, sometimes&nbsp;
even within a minute. Whoa.

00:01:58.640 --> 00:02:02.240
This new technique does a lot&nbsp;
of legwork that previous methods&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.240 --> 00:02:08.240
were not able to pull off so well. For instance,&nbsp;
it takes a little information about which part&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.240 --> 00:02:14.720
is in front or behind the model. Then, it&nbsp;
stitches all of these strokes together and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.720 --> 00:02:21.840
inflates our drawing into a 3D model, and it&nbsp;
does this better than previous methods, look.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.720 --> 00:02:29.040
Well, okay, the new one looks a bit better where&nbsp;
the body parts connect here, and that’s it?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.080 --> 00:02:36.080
Wait a second…a ha! Somebody&nbsp;
didn’t do their job correctly.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.040 --> 00:02:44.160
And we went from this work to this, in just&nbsp;
two years. This progress is absolute insanity.

00:02:44.960 --> 00:02:50.800
Now let’s have a look at a full workflow from&nbsp;
start to end. First, we draw the strokes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.360 --> 00:02:57.600
note that we can specify that one arm and leg is&nbsp;
in front of the body, and the other is behind,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.160 --> 00:03:06.960
and, bam the 3D model is now done! Wow, that was&nbsp;
quick. And now, add the little red control points&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.960 --> 00:03:15.360
for animation, and let the fun begin! Mister, your&nbsp;
paper has been officially accepted! Move the feet,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.360 --> 00:03:21.920
pin the hands, rock the body. Wait. Not only&nbsp;
that, but this paper was accepted to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.920 --> 00:03:27.360
SIGGRAPH ASIA conference, which is equivalent&nbsp;
to winning an olympic gold medal in computer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.360 --> 00:03:34.000
graphics research if you will, so add a little&nbsp;
neck movement too. Oh yeah! Now we’re talking!

00:03:34.800 --> 00:03:41.600
With this technique, the possibilities really feel&nbsp;
endless. We can animate humanoids, monsters, other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.600 --> 00:03:48.640
adorable creatures, or can even make scientific&nbsp;
illustrations come to life without any modeling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.640 --> 00:03:54.480
and rigging expertise. Do you remember this&nbsp;
earlier video where we could paint on a piece of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.480 --> 00:04:02.800
3D geometry, and transfer its properties onto a 3D&nbsp;
model? This method can be combined with that too.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.240 --> 00:04:14.960
Yum! And in case you are wondering how quick this&nbsp;
combination is - my goodness. Very, very quick.

00:04:15.840 --> 00:04:20.240
Now, this technique is also not perfect,&nbsp;
one of the limitations of this single-view&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.240 --> 00:04:26.560
drawing workflow is that we have only limited&nbsp;
control over the proportions in depth. Texturing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.560 --> 00:04:32.080
occluded regions is also not that easy. The&nbsp;
authors proposed possible solutions to these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.080 --> 00:04:37.680
limitations in the paper, so make sure to have a&nbsp;
look in the video description, and it appears to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.680 --> 00:04:43.760
me that with a little polishing, this may be&nbsp;
ready to go for artistic projects right now.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.320 --> 00:04:49.200
If you have a closer look, you will also&nbsp;
see that this work also cites the flow paper&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.200 --> 00:04:52.720
from Mihaly Csikszentmihalyi.&nbsp;
Extra style points for that.

00:04:53.360 --> 00:04:55.840
And with that said, when can we use this?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.480 --> 00:05:02.640
And that’s the best part - right now! The authors&nbsp;
really put their papers where their mouth is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.640 --> 00:05:08.320
or in other words, the source code for&nbsp;
this project is available, also, there is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.320 --> 00:05:14.800
an online demo. Wo-hoo! The link is available&nbsp;
in the video description, make sure to read the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.800 --> 00:05:21.360
instructions before you start. So there you&nbsp;
go, instant 3D models with animation without&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.360 --> 00:05:27.520
requiring 3D modeling and rigging expertise. What&nbsp;
do you think? Let me know in the comments below!

00:06:27.760 --> 00:06:31.920
Thanks for watching and for your generous&nbsp;
support, and I'll see you next time!

